{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgthjNTDBEnVJu8iqjqgm","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Correctly scaled, the logit lens gives a clear and more specific picture. Up to the input of the last layer, the copy margin grows similarly on ordinary text and inside loops (hidden_20: 4.3 pre-loop, 5.3 at cycle 0, 5.4 late). Two jumps come from layers with induction heads (layer 15: +1 to 1.5; layer 19: +1). The final layer (23) then behaves very differently by context: it slightly lowers the margin on ordinary text (5.53 → 4.97) and at the loop's first cycle (5.98 → 5.74), but adds about +5 nats in late cycles (7.80 → 12.75). So the last layer is a loop-specific copy amplifier, consistent with its induction head H5 attending 0.74 in loops versus 0.28 pre-loop. Pass 7 already showed that making loops escapable by ablating copy heads did not help: the model escaped and re-looped, and normal copying suffered. So the root problem is the stuck re-verification state that loops crystallize from, not the copy machinery itself. The brake worked because it acted at that state's native decision point (the doubt token at a line start). I will check whether the surviving restatement loops have a comparably dominant line-start decision, such as re-check openers like \"Let's check\" or \"Let's try\". If they do, an analogous native component may control it.\n\n```python\n# Hypothesis: surviving x2 restatement loops are dominated by a few line-start openers (e.g. \"Let's check/try\", re-quoting), i.e. a re-check decision.\nfrom collections import Counter\ndef line_openers(unit):\n    outs = []\n    for ln in unit.split('\\n')[1:]:\n        s = ln.strip().lstrip('*').strip()\n        if s: outs.append(' '.join(s.split()[:2])[:20])\n    return outs\nc11, c2_ = Counter(), Counter()\nfor u in LU11[~LU11.doubt].unit: c11.update(set(line_openers(u)))\nfor u in LU2.unit: c2_.update(set(line_openers(u)))\nprint('x2 non-doubt loops n=', (~LU11.doubt).sum(), 'top openers (units containing):', [(asc_(k), v) for k, v in c11.most_common(18)])\nprint('orig loops n=', len(LU2), 'top openers:', [(asc_(k), v) for k, v in c2_.most_common(12)])\nfirst_word = Counter(w.split()[0] for u in LU11[~LU11.doubt].unit for w in set(line_openers(u)) if w)\nprint('x2 first words:', [(asc_(k), v) for k, v in first_word.most_common(15)])\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4739,"cache_read_input_tokens":76202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4739},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b4bcd242-3053-49df-a015-76e7a59477b0","uuid":"9321a47a-463b-452e-85a3-a738f7cf6ce3","timestamp":"2026-11-10T05:34:49.579Z","request_id":"req_011CgthjMTvUxC9t4QEsXhiC"}
